Done. Both files written (`impl/d1_prime.c`, `strategies/d1_prime.md`); everything measured on the a80n0 scoring node under the live reservation (the wallaby squeue shim still lies about job 440424 — worked around with the r3 personal-shim trick).

**Round summary:**
- **Technique ended on: c-seed folding** — the map chain's additive c-field folds into the accumulator *seeds* (P seeded with (cF+cG)/2, S with (cF−cG)/2), removing all per-step c-adds from the serial path; applied to every chain path, plus new intrinsic B=1 chains for 13/31 that take back d1_batchlane's refinements of my r1 design (all-ones x0 row, targeted barrier, their 1e-100 clamp finding), and a two-transform tiling of the pair kernel for batched 31 (table rows loaded once per pair, broadcasts moved to the load ports).
- **Interleaved same-window gains vs r3:** 31 B=1 chain 0.059→0.054 µs (+9%, takes the cell from batchlane), 13 B=1 chain 0.039→0.037 (+5%, takes the cell), 13 B=512 chain +2.8%, 31 B=512 chain +3.6%, 31 B=512 m=1 +2–4%; m=1 cells untouched and unregressed.
- **What died, with numbers:** split accumulators at 31 (0.064–0.066 vs 0.061 — the single long FMA chain hides the late maps' latency), exact sqrt/div map (0.090 vs 0.061 at 31; divider queueing), vpermpd broadcasts at 13 (0.045 vs 0.043). Also confirmed by PMU that dense batched-31 sits at its port floor — Rader isn't worth it there.
- **Key process finding for the panel:** the scoring node itself drifts ~14% between windows — batchlane's board 0.0546 at 31 B=1 chain was a window artifact (0.062 same-window), and two of my own early conclusions reversed under interleaving. Interleave on the node, always.

One-line reply: **c-seed–folded register-resident chains + two-transform pair tiling; scoring-node (a80n0, wallaby unused — ICX is what's scored) best: B=1 0.018 µs at L=13 / 0.048 µs at L=31, batched 0.011 µs at L=13 / 0.043 µs at L=31 (chained: 0.037/0.054 B=1, 0.015/0.047 B=512); rel L2 1.7–3.1e-16 single-call, all chain gates pass with ≥3 decades of margin.**
